@@ -0,0 +1,170 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `borsh`
+
+Property-based tests were added to the `borsh` crate (near/borsh-rs @
+7fc21fe, v1.8.0) using [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2
+dev-dependency, added with `--ignore-rust-version`: borsh pins
+`rust-version = 1.77.0`, hegeltest needs 1.86 — flag this to maintainers if
+upstreaming). The pre-existing `fuzz/` target only checks that
+`deserialize` never panics on arbitrary bytes for std types, so these tests
+target what it does not: round-trips, canonicality (both directions),
+spec-conformant wire layout, and derived types.
+
+Tests live in the existing integration-test binary (`tests/tests.rs`),
+inside the files they belong to:
+
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/requires_derive_category/test_combined_properties.rs` (new module file, registered in `tests.rs`; 6 tests)
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/requires_derive_category/test_enum_discriminants.rs` (2 tests)
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/test_primitives.rs` (2 tests)
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/test_btree_map.rs` (2 tests)
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/test_hash_map.rs` (3 tests)
+- `borsh/tests/roundtrip/test_vecs.rs` (1 test)
+- `borsh/tests/deserialization_errors/test_initial.rs` (3 tests)
+
+## Properties tested
+
+The oracle throughout is the BORSH specification
+(near/borsh#specification, linked from the crate README as a "strict
+specification"), transcribed independently rather than trusting borsh's own
+encoder.
+
+1. **Rich derived-type round-trip** (`prop_rich_roundtrip`): `from_slice
+   (to_vec(x)) == x` for a `Rich` struct exercising all integer widths
+   (u8–u128, i8–i128), f32/f64 (non-NaN, including ±0.0/inf/subnormals),
+   bool, String, `Option<Box<enum>>`, a nested enum with explicit
+   discriminants (`= 2`, `= 9`, `= 17` under `use_discriminant = true`),
+   `Vec<enum>`, `Vec<u8>` (the `u8_slice` fast path), `[u16; 3]`, a tuple,
+   `BTreeMap<String, Vec<u8>>`, `BTreeSet<i32>`, `HashMap<u8, u32>`, and
+   `HashSet<i64>`.
+
+2. **Spec-transcribed wire-format oracle**
+   (`prop_rich_wire_format_matches_spec`): `to_vec(x)` equals an
+   independent encoder written from the spec (little-endian fixed-width
+   integers, IEEE-754 bits for floats, u32 length prefixes, 0/1 tags for
+   bool/Option, u8 enum tag equal to the *declared* discriminant, fixed
+   arrays without length prefix, and — the classic bug spot — HashMap/
+   HashSet entries in strictly ascending key order, sorted independently by
+   the oracle).
+
+3. **`object_length` consistency**
+   (`prop_rich_object_length_matches_serialized_len`):
+   `object_length(&x) == to_vec(&x).len()`.
+
+4. **Injectivity (canonicality, encode direction)**
+   (`prop_rich_encoding_is_injective`): distinct values produce distinct
+   bytes; generation is biased toward single-field mutations of one value
+   to concentrate on near-collisions.
+
+5. **Strict prefixes rejected** (`prop_rich_strict_prefix_rejected`): every
+   strict prefix of a valid encoding fails `from_slice` (the format is
+   deterministic, so encodings cannot be prefixes of each other).
+
+6. **Canonical acceptance (validation/rejection direction)**
+   (`prop_plain_accepted_bytes_are_canonical`): for a map-free derived type
+   (maps are the one documented non-strict spot, see below), any byte
+   string the deserializer *accepts* — inputs are single-byte corruptions
+   of valid encodings plus arbitrary bytes — must re-serialize to exactly
+   the same bytes, and decoding must never panic.
+
+7. **Enum tag = declared discriminant + roundtrip**
+   (`prop_enum_tag_is_declared_discriminant_and_roundtrips`): reuses the
+   existing `XY` test enum (discriminants 0, 20, 21, 22, 10, 11) with
+   generated payloads; first wire byte equals the declared discriminant.
+
+8. **Undeclared enum tags rejected** (`prop_undeclared_enum_tag_rejected`):
+   any tag byte outside the declared set errs, regardless of trailing
+   bytes — non-minimal/gap discriminants are not silently mapped.
+
+9. **Float bit-exact round-trip + LE layout**
+   (`prop_f32_f64_bit_exact_roundtrip_and_le_layout`): drawn from raw bit
+   patterns; non-NaN floats round-trip with identical bits and encode as
+   the LE bytes of `to_bits()`.
+
+10. **NaN rejected on both sides** (`prop_nan_rejected_on_both_sides`):
+    every constructed NaN bit pattern (sign × non-zero mantissa) fails
+    `to_vec`, and its byte image fails `from_slice` — borsh's documented
+    portability rule.
+
+11. **Bool/Option/Result canonical tags** (`prop_bool_byte_canonical`,
+    `prop_option_tag_canonical`, `prop_result_tag_canonical`): byte 0/1
+    decode to the corresponding value; every other byte is rejected, never
+    coerced.
+
+12. **Map/set key-order enforcement**
+    (`prop_btreemap_key_order_enforcement`,
+    `prop_btreeset_item_order_enforcement`,
+    `prop_hashmap_key_order_enforcement`): hand-encoded entry sequences
+    (biased half toward sorted-unique). Under `de_strict_order`, decoding
+    succeeds iff keys are strictly ascending; under default features the
+    documented lenient behavior is pinned (accepts any order, last value
+    wins for duplicates, matching sequential-insert semantics). Both cfg
+    branches were executed (see Test results).
+
+13. **Hash/BTree sibling agreement** (`prop_hashmap_serializes_like_btreemap`,
+    `prop_hashset_serializes_like_btreeset`): the same entries serialized
+    through `HashMap`/`HashSet` (which must sort) and through
+    `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` (ordered iteration — an independent sorting path)
+    produce identical bytes.
+
+14. **VecDeque ring-buffer path** (`prop_rotated_vecdeque_serializes_like_vec`):
+    a rotated `VecDeque` (non-trivial `as_slices()` split, exercising the
+    two-part `serialize_slice` path) serializes identically to a `Vec` in
+    the same iteration order, and round-trips.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test -p borsh --features derive`: all pass (164 in `tests.rs`,
+  19 of them hegel properties; plus lib/common/smoke).
+- `cargo test -p borsh --features derive,de_strict_order`: all pass —
+  exercises the strict branches of the key-order properties.
+- `cargo test --workspace` (default features, no derive): all pass.
+- Liveness check: temporarily inverting the discriminant assertion produced
+  an immediate shrunk counterexample (`draw_1 = 0`, i.e. `XY::A`); reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000`, mutation test 3000) under both
+  feature sets: all 19 properties pass. Case counts were restored to
+  defaults for the committed tests.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Notable non-bug observations:
+
+- **Default-features maps break bijectivity by design**: without
+  `de_strict_order`, out-of-order and duplicate map/set keys are silently
+  accepted and reordered/deduplicated. This is a deliberate, feature-gated
+  design choice already pinned by borsh's own `*_wrong_order_test!` macro
+  tests, so it is documented and asserted (both branches) rather than
+  reported as a bug.
+- Signed zero: `-0.0 == 0.0` in Rust but the encodings differ (distinct bit
+  patterns). The injectivity property only asserts `x != y ⇒ bytes differ`,
+  so this does not trip it; borsh serializes bit patterns, which is
+  spec-conformant.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- An early draft of the canonical-acceptance property (#6) over the full
+  `Rich` type would be unsound under default features: a one-byte mutation
+  that swaps map key order decodes fine (leniently) but re-encodes sorted.
+  Restricted to a map-free `Plain` type so it holds unconditionally; map
+  canonicality is covered separately by property #12 with explicit cfg
+  branches.
+- The tag-0 branch of the Option property originally asserted
+  `Ok(None)`; with a payload appended, `from_slice` correctly rejects the
+  trailing bytes ("Not all bytes read"), so that branch asserts rejection
+  and `None` round-trip is asserted separately.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's guidance held up: `--ignore-rust-version` for the
+  MSRV mismatch was needed exactly as documented; `generators::arrays`
+  size-by-inference and `tuples!` worked as described.
+- The "spec, transcribed" oracle pattern from Oracle Sourcing was the
+  highest-value tool here; combined with the sibling-implementation oracle
+  (HashMap vs BTreeMap) it covered both the sorted-serialization mandate
+  and the layout without ever trusting borsh's own encoder.
+- Feature-gated behavior (`de_strict_order`) forces properties with cfg
+  branches; the existing macro tests in `common_macro.rs` provided a good
+  template. A skill note on "when the contract itself is feature-gated,
+  assert both branches under cfg" might generalize.
+- The test crate is conditionally `no_std`
+  (`#![cfg_attr(not(feature = "std"), no_std)]` + `extern crate alloc`);
+  gating hegel modules on `#[cfg(feature = "std")]` was enough — no
+  `extern crate std` tricks needed since std is a default feature.
